<!-- ══════════════════════════════════ ARTICLE ══════════════════ -->
<article class="post-body">

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §1 The Long Horizon -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Long-Horizon Bottleneck</div>
<h2>Why data science breaks standard agents</h2>
<p>
  Autonomous data science is inherently exploratory, iterative, and long-horizon. An agent must load massive datasets, clean missing entries, explore feature relationships, write code, run model trials, inspect stack traces, and debug execution errors. This multi-stage feedback loop produces two fundamental problems for standard single-agent architectures:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Static Action Spaces:</strong> Standard agents operate with a predefined, hardcoded list of tool primitives. When a task requires highly customized handling (such as class-imbalanced resampling or advanced model fine-tuning), the agent cannot synthesize new tools. It is stuck in a loop of trial-and-error using generic primitives.</li>
  <li><strong>Context Explosion:</strong> Every trial output, traceback log, and pandas printout is appended directly to the prompt. In standard agents, context grows linearly and uncontrollably until it hits model token limits, triggering Out-of-Token (OOT) failures or inducing severe reasoning degradation (the "lost-in-the-middle" effect).</li>
</ul>

